(_jb1.chapters = _jb1.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Job, a wealthy man in the land of Uz, is described as blameless, upright, God-fearing, and careful to avoid evil. He has seven sons, three daughters, and vast herds, and he regularly offers sacrifices in case his children have sinned in their hearts. In heaven, Satan appears before the Lord and challenges Job's motives: Job serves God only because God protects and prospers him, and if everything is taken away he will curse God openly. The Lord grants Satan power over Job's possessions but not over his body. Four messengers arrive in rapid succession: raiders steal the oxen and donkeys, fire from heaven destroys the sheep, Chaldean bands carry off the camels, and a wind collapses the house on all ten of Job's children. Job tears his robe, shaves his head, worships, and refuses to charge God with wrongdoing.",
    context:"The book's opening frame introduces Job in the land of Uz; authorship and date are uncertain, though the setting is traditionally placed in the patriarchal age, and this scene sets up Satan's challenge.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 1:8", text:"And the LORD said unto Satan, Hast thou considered my servant Job, that there is none like him in the earth, a perfect and an upright man, one that feareth God, and escheweth evil?" },
      { ref:"Job 1:21", text:"And said, Naked came I out of my mother's womb, and naked shall I return thither: the LORD gave, and the LORD hath taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD." },
      { ref:"Job 1:22", text:"In all this Job sinned not, nor charged God foolishly." }
    ],
    takeaway:"What the Lord gives, the Lord may take away; worship that blesses His name in loss proves faith is more than a bargain.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many sons and daughters had been born to Job?", choices:["Seven sons and three daughters","Three sons and seven daughters","Ten sons and no daughters","Five sons and five daughters"], a:0, why:"Job 1:2 records seven sons and three daughters born to Job." },
      { q:"What claim did Satan make about why Job feared God?", choices:["Job served God because he was naturally better than other men","Job feared God only because God had blessed and protected everything he did","Job worshiped God out of terror of another flood","Job only pretended to be religious to impress the men of the east"], a:1, why:"Satan argued that God had made a hedge about Job and blessed the work of his hands, so Job's devotion was bought with prosperity." },
      { q:"What does Job's response to losing his children and wealth teach about worship?", choices:["Worship should stop when blessings stop","Grief must be hidden so others cannot see it","True faith blesses God even when He takes away what He gave","Loss should be explained immediately as punishment"], a:2, why:"Job tore his mantle, fell on the ground, and worshiped, saying the Lord gave and the Lord had taken away; he charged God not foolishly." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Satan appears before the Lord again. God points out that Job still holds to his integrity even though Satan stirred Him to destroy Job without cause. Satan answers that people will surrender everything to protect their own lives, so if Job's bone and flesh are struck, he will curse God to his face. The Lord allows this but spares Job's life. A terrible eruption of boils covers Job from his feet to his head; he scrapes himself with a broken piece of pottery and sits in the ashes. His wife tells him to abandon his integrity, curse God, and die, but Job refuses to sin with his lips. His three friends — Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar — arrive to comfort him, weep at his condition, and sit with him in silence for a full week.",
    context:"Round two of the heavenly challenge: Satan strikes Job's body, his wife wavers, and the three friends — Eliphaz, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite — arrive to mourn with him.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 2:5", text:"But put forth thine hand now, and touch his bone and his flesh, and he will curse thee to thy face." },
      { ref:"Job 2:10", text:"But he said unto her, Thou speakest as one of the foolish women speaketh. What? shall we receive good at the hand of God, and shall we not receive evil? In all this did not Job sin with his lips." },
      { ref:"Job 2:13", text:"So they sat down with him upon the ground seven days and seven nights, and none spake a word unto him: for they saw that his grief was very great." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Integrity means holding to God when the test moves from possessions to pain, and accepting both good and evil from His hand.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How long did Job's three friends sit with him without speaking?", choices:["Three days and three nights","Seven days and seven nights","Forty days and forty nights","One full month"], a:1, why:"They sat on the ground with him seven days and seven nights, for they saw that his grief was very great." },
      { q:"What did Satan mean by saying 'Skin for skin, yea, all that a man hath will he give for his life'?", choices:["People willingly trade injuries to defend their honor","Wealth matters more to a man than his family","A man will give up everything he owns to protect his own life","Friends should repay each wound with equal revenge"], a:2, why:"Satan claimed Job's piety held while his possessions were safe, but would break the moment his own body and life were threatened." },
      { q:"What principle does Job's answer to his wife teach?", choices:["Spouses should avoid hard conversations during grief","Only good things should be expected from God's hand","Silence is the only faithful response to loss","Faith accepts both good and adversity from God's hand without cursing Him"], a:3, why:"Job asked, shall we receive good at the hand of God and not receive evil, and in all this did not sin with his lips." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"After a week of silence, Job finally speaks — and what he says is not praise but a curse on the day of his birth. He wishes that day swallowed in darkness, removed from the calendar, and cursed by those ready to stir up their mourning, because it let him live to see sorrow. He asks why he did not die at birth, since at least the grave offers rest: there kings, princes, prisoners, and slaves alike lie quiet, and the weary are free from their tormentors. Job then asks why light and life are given to people in bitter misery who long for death more than for buried treasure. He ends by confessing that the disaster he always dreaded has now arrived, that his sighing is constant, and that he never had real security — yet trouble came anyway.",
    context:"After seven days of shared silence, Job breaks the quiet with the first speech of the poem: a bitter lament cursing the day of his birth.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 3:17", text:"There the wicked cease from troubling; and there the weary be at rest." },
      { ref:"Job 3:20", text:"Wherefore is light given to him that is in misery, and life unto the bitter in soul;" },
      { ref:"Job 3:25", text:"For the thing which I greatly feared is come upon me, and that which I was afraid of is come unto me." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Honest grief may be brought to God in full; lament is not the same as cursing Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Whom did Job wish could curse the day of his birth?", choices:["His three friends","The priests of the land","Them that curse the day, who are ready to raise up their mourning","The men of the east"], a:2, why:"Job called on them that curse the day to rouse their mourning against his birthday, wishing it blotted from the calendar." },
      { q:"Why did Job wish he had died at birth, according to his reasoning in this chapter?", choices:["He wanted to avoid further wealth","He feared his servants would betray him","He believed he had committed an unforgivable sin","In the grave the wicked cease from troubling and the weary are at rest"], a:3, why:"Job pictured the grave as a place of rest where prisoners hear not the oppressor, the servant is free from his master, and the weary are at rest." },
      { q:"What truth does Job's lament in this chapter model for the faithful?", choices:["Honest sorrow can be voiced before God rather than denied","Faithful people are exempt from despair","Death should be sought whenever life becomes hard","Suffering always proves hidden sin"], a:0, why:"Job cursed his day and wished for death, yet he poured all of it out before God; the poem shows lament voiced to Him without abandoning Him." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Eliphaz the Temanite speaks first for the friends. He gently reminds Job that he once strengthened others with wise words, yet now collapses when trouble touches himself. Then comes his core belief: no innocent person has ever perished, so suffering must be harvest from seeds of wickedness. Eliphaz claims private confirmation — a trembling night vision in which a spirit glided past him and asked whether any mortal could be more just or pure than his Maker. Humans, living in houses of clay crushed before the moth, cannot expect exemption from trouble, and even angels are charged with folly in God's sight. The speech implies Job's suffering is exactly what hidden sin deserves, though Eliphaz states it here as a general principle rather than a direct accusation.",
    context:"The first cycle of speeches opens as Eliphaz the Temanite answers Job, appealing to his own night vision to defend the rule that suffering follows sin.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 4:7", text:"Remember, I pray thee, who ever perished, being innocent? or where were the righteous cut off?" },
      { ref:"Job 4:8", text:"Even as I have seen, they that plow iniquity, and sow wickedness, reap the same." },
      { ref:"Job 4:17", text:"Shall mortal man be more just than God? shall a man be more pure than his maker?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Sin has consequences, but a sufferer's pain is not automatic proof of secret guilt.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What experience did Eliphaz describe to back up his teaching?", choices:["A visit to a temple oracle","A dream sent to his father","A conversation with Job's servants","A trembling night vision in which a spirit passed before his face"], a:3, why:"Eliphaz said that in thoughts from the visions of the night, a spirit passed before his face and a voice declared no man just before God." },
      { q:"What was the core principle of Eliphaz's argument?", choices:["The innocent never perish; suffering is the harvest of sown wickedness","God afflicts the righteous to test their loyalty","Suffering is random and teaches nothing","Prosperity proves nothing about a person's standing"], a:0, why:"He asked who ever perished being innocent, and said they that plow iniquity and sow wickedness reap the same." },
      { q:"What did the voice in Eliphaz's vision affirm about humanity before God?", choices:["Mortals can out-argue their Maker","Man cannot be more just or pure than God who made him","God owes each person an explanation","Angels and men share equal standing"], a:1, why:"The voice asked, shall mortal man be more just than God, and described men as frail dwellers in houses of clay." }
    ] },
  { n:5, summary:"Eliphaz continues his counsel. Resentment, he warns, kills the foolish man, for trouble is humanity's natural condition, like sparks flying upward from a fire. The right response is to seek God, who works unsearchable wonders, sends rain on the earth, lifts the lowly, frustrates the schemes of the crafty, and rescues the poor. Then comes his famous, gentle-sounding summary: happy is the person God corrects, for the Almighty both wounds and heals. Eliphaz promises that if Job accepts the discipline, God will deliver him from famine, war, slander, and destruction; he will laugh at fear, live in peace, enjoy prosperous descendants, and die old and satisfied, like grain harvested in its season. He insists this teaching has been thoroughly searched and is certain.",
    context:"Eliphaz continues his first speech, turning from the vision's warning to counsel: accept God's correction and prosperity will return.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 5:7", text:"Yet man is born unto trouble, as the sparks fly upward." },
      { ref:"Job 5:17", text:"Behold, happy is the man whom God correcteth: therefore despise not thou the chastening of the Almighty:" },
      { ref:"Job 5:18", text:"For he maketh sore, and bindeth up: he woundeth, and his hands make whole." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Do not despise the Almighty's correction; the God who wounds is the God whose hands make whole.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"According to Eliphaz, man is born unto trouble, as what flies upward?", choices:["The sparks","The eagle","The chaff","The smoke of a furnace"], a:0, why:"Eliphaz declared that affliction does not spring from the ground, yet man is born unto trouble as the sparks fly upward." },
      { q:"What did Eliphaz counsel Job to do in his affliction?", choices:["Protest his innocence before the elders","Seek God, commit his cause to Him, and accept His correction","Take vengeance on the raiders who destroyed his flocks","Withdraw into silence and wait for death"], a:1, why:"He said, I would seek unto God, and happy is the man whom God correcteth; therefore despise not the chastening of the Almighty." },
      { q:"What blessings did Eliphaz promise if Job accepted God's correction?", choices:["Immediate restoration of all lost children and herds","Exemption from every future trial","Deliverance through troubles, peace at home, posterity, and a full-age death","Revenge against all who wronged him"], a:2, why:"Eliphaz promised deliverance in six and seven troubles, security in his tabernacle, offspring like the grass of the earth, and death in full age like a shock of corn." }
    ] },
  { n:6, summary:"Job answers Eliphaz directly. If his grief could be weighed, he says, it would be heavier than the sand of the sea, for God's arrows have poisoned his spirit. Like a donkey crying when it has no grass, his complaint has an obvious cause: he longs for God to end his life, because he has not concealed the words of the Holy One. His strength is gone, and he expects death. Then he turns on his friends: to a suffering man, pity from a friend is owed, but they have proved as unreliable as desert streams that vanish in heat. Caravans that counted on such water failed and were ashamed. Job insists he never asked them for money or rescue — only for honest teaching. Right words have force, he says; their arguments prove nothing except that they would dig a pit for their friend.",
    context:"Job's first recorded reply answers Eliphaz: his complaint has a cause, and his friends have failed the basic duty of pity.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 6:2", text:"Oh that my grief were throughly weighed, and my calamity laid in the balances together!" },
      { ref:"Job 6:14", text:"To him that is afflicted pity should be shewed from his friend; but he forsaketh the fear of the Almighty." },
      { ref:"Job 6:24", text:"Teach me, and I will hold my tongue: and cause me to understand wherein I have erred." }
    ],
    takeaway:"A suffering friend needs pity before instruction; presence and kindness carry more weight than arguments.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What natural image did Job use to describe his friends' unreliability?", choices:["A spider's web","A desert brook that vanishes in heat","A weaver's shuttle","A broken potsherd"], a:1, why:"Job compared his brethren to brooks that wax warm, vanish, and disappoint the troops of Tema and companies of Sheba that hoped in them." },
      { q:"What did Job say a suffering person deserves from friends?", choices:["Silence until the trial ends","Prayers offered at a distance","Pity and kindness shown to the afflicted","Material restitution for his losses"], a:2, why:"Job said to him that is afflicted pity should be showed from his friend, and accused them of seeing his casting down and being afraid." },
      { q:"What does Job's request in this chapter model for anyone corrected or criticized?", choices:["Refuse to hear any rebuke","Pay critics back with sharper words","End friendships over disagreement","Ask to be shown specifically where one has erred"], a:3, why:"Job said, teach me, and I will hold my tongue: and cause me to understand wherein I have erred." }
    ] },
  { n:7, summary:"Job describes the misery of his days. Life is hard service: like a hired worker longing for payday, he owns months of futility and sleepless, tossing nights. His flesh is wormy and foul, his days speed past like a weaver's shuttle, spent without hope, and anyone who goes down to the grave returns no more. He will not restrain his mouth; he will complain in the bitterness of his soul. He asks whether he is the sea or a whale, that God should set a watch over him, since even his bed frightens him with dreams and visions until he prefers death to life. He asks what man is that God should magnify him, set his heart on him, and try him every moment; he begs for a brief release, admits he has sinned, and asks why God will not pardon his transgression before he sleeps in the dust and is sought no more.",
    context:"Job continues his reply to Eliphaz, pressing his case directly toward God: life feels like endless forced labor under constant watch.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 7:1", text:"Is there not an appointed time to man upon earth? are not his days also like the days of an hireling?" },
      { ref:"Job 7:11", text:"Therefore I will not refrain my mouth; I will speak in the anguish of my spirit; I will complain in the bitterness of my soul." },
      { ref:"Job 7:17", text:"What is man, that thou shouldest magnify him? and that thou shouldest set thine heart upon him?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"God can bear our bitterest questions; He invites honesty, not rehearsed serenity.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"To what did Job compare his days in this chapter?", choices:["A slow-moving caravan","A lamp burning in darkness","A weaver's shuttle","A mountain falling"], a:2, why:"Job said his days are swifter than a weaver's shuttle and are spent without hope." },
      { q:"What did Job ask God to do for him in his misery?", choices:["Explain the mysteries of the stars to him","Restore his health within the week","Send a prophet with a message","Depart from him and let him alone until he could swallow down his spittle"], a:3, why:"Overwhelmed by terrifying dreams and constant trials, Job begged God not to visit and try him every moment but to depart and let him alone for a little." },
      { q:"What does Job's frank complaint in this chapter show about prayer?", choices:["God allows His children to speak their anguish honestly to Him","Prayer must always begin with praise","Complaining to God is unpardonable","Only priests may address God in bitterness"], a:0, why:"Job declared he would not refrain his mouth but would speak in the anguish of his spirit and complain in the bitterness of his soul." }
    ] },
  { n:8, summary:"Bildad the Shuhite answers bluntly. How long, he asks, will Job's words blow like a strong wind? God never perverts justice, so if Job's children died, they must have sinned; and if Job himself will earnestly seek God and be pure and upright, God will awake, restore him, and make his latter end greater than his small beginning. For support Bildad appeals to the wisdom of past generations: we are but of yesterday, but the fathers can teach us. His illustrations are severe — plants that grow without water wither fast, and the hopes of those who forget God perish like a spider's web, while a house leaning on that hope will not stand. Yet he ends on hope: God will not cast away a perfect man, and will one day fill Job's mouth with laughter.",
    context:"Bildad the Shuhite, the second friend, makes his first speech, grounding retribution theology in the inherited wisdom of past generations.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 8:3", text:"Doth God pervert judgment? or doth the Almighty pervert justice?" },
      { ref:"Job 8:13", text:"So are the paths of all that forget God; and the hypocrite's hope shall perish:" },
      { ref:"Job 8:20", text:"Behold, God will not cast away a perfect man, neither will he help the evil doers:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"The hypocrite's hope perishes like a spider's web, but God will not cast away the upright.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where did Bildad tell Job to look for wisdom to understand his situation?", choices:["The stars and their patterns","His own inner feelings","The priests at the altar","The former age and the wisdom of the fathers"], a:3, why:"Bildad urged Job to inquire of the former age and search what their fathers taught, for we are but of yesterday and know nothing." },
      { q:"How did Bildad explain the death of Job's children?", choices:["They had sinned and God cast them away for their transgression","A disease common to the region took them","They were slain by raiders for their father's debts","God never takes children for a father's sake"], a:0, why:"Bildad said, if thy children have sinned against him, and he have cast them away for their transgression — implying their deaths were deserved punishment." },
      { q:"What did Bildad promise if Job were pure and upright?", choices:["A sign from heaven confirming his innocence","God would awake for him and make his latter end greater than his beginning","Immediate recall of his children to life","Vindication before the men of the east"], a:1, why:"Bildad said if thou wert pure and upright, surely now he would awake for thee, and though thy beginning was small, thy latter end should greatly increase." }
    ] },
  { n:9, summary:"Job concedes Bildad's premise but presses the point: how can a man be just with God? No one could answer God once in a thousand questions; the one who removes mountains, shakes the earth, commands the sun, spreads out the heavens, and names the constellations cannot be seen passing by, and no one can hinder Him or ask what He is doing. Even if Job were righteous, he would only beg his Judge for mercy, for God breaks him with a tempest and multiplies wounds without cause. Job's sharpest protest follows: God destroys the blameless and the wicked alike, lets the scourge mock the innocent, and hands the earth over to the wicked. Washing himself in snow water would change nothing, for God is not a man Job could face in court. There is no daysman between them to lay a hand on both.",
    context:"Job concedes God's power and justice but argues that no man, righteous or not, can win a case against Him — and wishes for an umpire.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 9:2", text:"I know it is so of a truth: but how should man be just with God?" },
      { ref:"Job 9:22", text:"This is one thing, therefore I said it, He destroyeth the perfect and the wicked." },
      { ref:"Job 9:33", text:"Neither is there any daysman betwixt us, that might lay his hand upon us both." }
    ],
    takeaway:"No one can stand before God on personal merit; we need a mediator to lay His hand on us both.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which constellations did Job name as God's handiwork?", choices:["Arcturus, Orion, and Pleiades","Cassiopeia, Draco, and Lyra","Leo, Cancer, and Libra","The Bear, the Dragon, and the Cross"], a:0, why:"Job said God maketh Arcturus, Orion, and Pleiades, and the chambers of the south." },
      { q:"What did Job admit in answering Bildad, and what question did he press?", choices:["He denied God's power and asked if he could seize control","He agreed God is mighty, but asked how man can be just with God","He confessed hidden crimes and asked for a priest","He rejected the friends and asked them to leave"], a:1, why:"Job said, I know it is so of a truth: but how should man be just with God? No one could answer Him one of a thousand." },
      { q:"What does Job's cry for a 'daysman' reveal about the human condition?", choices:["People prefer lawsuits to friendship","Justice is impossible in this world","Man needs a mediator able to lay his hand on both him and God","Only the wealthy can afford legal defense"], a:2, why:"Job said God is not a man as he was, and neither is there any daysman betwixt us, that might lay his hand upon us both." }
    ] },
  { n:10, summary:"Job continues straight to God. Weary of life, he asks the Lord not to condemn him but to show why He contends with him. Does it profit God to oppress the work of His own hands, to see as man sees, to hunt for Job's sin though He knows he is not wicked? Job then recalls how God made him — fashioning him like clay, pouring him out like milk, clothing him with skin and flesh, granting him life, favor, and preserving care — and asks why a Maker would now destroy what He built. If he sins, God marks it; if he is righteous, he dare not lift his head; he is hunted like a lion's prey. He wishes he had never been born, and asks God simply to leave him alone long enough for a little comfort before he goes to the land of darkness.",
    context:"Job continues the same address to God, pleading his Maker's own investment in him and asking why he was ever born.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 10:2", text:"I will say unto God, Do not condemn me; shew me wherefore thou contendest with me." },
      { ref:"Job 10:8", text:"Thine hands have made me and fashioned me together round about; yet thou dost destroy me." },
      { ref:"Job 10:12", text:"Thou hast granted me life and favour, and thy visitation hath preserved my spirit." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When God's purposes are hidden, we may still bring our case to Him directly and ask Him to show us why.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What images did Job use to describe how God formed him?", choices:["Stone carved and timber joined","Clay fashioned and milk curdled like cheese","Silver refined and gold cast","Weaving on a loom and writing on tablets"], a:1, why:"Job said God made him as the clay, poured him out as milk, and curdled him like cheese, then clothed him with skin and flesh." },
      { q:"What two requests did Job make of God at the opening of this speech?", choices:["End his life and bury him with honor","Give back his herds and rebuild his house","Do not condemn him, and show him why God contended with him","Punish his enemies and bless his friends"], a:2, why:"Job said, I will say unto God, Do not condemn me; shew me wherefore thou contendest with me." },
      { q:"What does Job's bold questioning in this chapter model for believers in unexplained suffering?", choices:["Silence is the only reverent response","Blame the friends who visit you","Assume God has abandoned you","Take the question of God's purposes directly to God Himself"], a:3, why:"Job addressed his confusion straight to God — asking why his Maker destroyed what He fashioned — rather than walking away from Him." }
    ] },
  { n:11, summary:"Zophar the Naamathite, the third friend, is the harshest yet. All this multitude of words, he says, deserves an answer; Job's claims of pure doctrine and cleanness are lies that should shame him. If only God would speak, He would reveal the secrets of wisdom and show Job that he is actually getting less than his sin deserves. Zophar preaches God's incomprehensibility: the Almighty is higher than heaven and deeper than hell, and vain man — born like a wild donkey's colt — cannot find Him out by searching. Yet the path forward is simple, he insists: prepare your heart, stretch out your hands, and put away the iniquity in your tent. Then Job would lift up his face without spot, forget his misery, shine like the morning, and rest secure — while the eyes of the wicked fail.",
    context:"Zophar the Naamathite, the third and bluntest friend, opens his first speech by mocking Job's words and demanding his repentance.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 11:7", text:"Canst thou by searching find out God? canst thou find out the Almighty unto perfection?" },
      { ref:"Job 11:13", text:"If thou prepare thine heart, and stretch out thine hands toward him;" },
      { ref:"Job 11:17", text:"And thine age shall be clearer than the noonday; thou shalt shine forth, thou shalt be as the morning." }
    ],
    takeaway:"We cannot find out the Almighty by searching, but hearts prepared toward Him find rest and hope.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How did Zophar describe the reach of God's wisdom?", choices:["Narrower than a city gate","Confined to the land of Uz","Longer than the earth and broader than the sea","Measurable by the number of the stars"], a:2, why:"Zophar said the wisdom of God is as high as heaven and deeper than hell, its measure longer than the earth and broader than the sea." },
      { q:"What provocative claim did Zophar make about Job's suffering?", choices:["Job's suffering was a dream and would soon vanish","Job's real problem was his disloyal servants","Job's suffering had no meaning at all","God was exacting of Job less than his iniquity deserved"], a:3, why:"Zophar said God would show Job the secrets of wisdom, and that God exacteth of thee less than thine iniquity deserveth." },
      { q:"What does Zophar's question about searching out God teach?", choices:["God cannot be fully found out by human searching, so humility is fitting","Clever people can decode all of God's ways","God hides Himself to frustrate the faithful","The Almighty is knowable only to the aged"], a:0, why:"Zophar asked, canst thou by searching find out God? canst thou find out the Almighty unto perfection?" }
    ] },
  { n:12, summary:"Job replies with heavy irony. No doubt, he says, you are the people, and wisdom will die with you — but he has understanding too, and what they preach is common knowledge. The real world mocks their formula: the just man is laughed to scorn while robbers prosper and those who provoke God live secure. Even beasts, birds, earth, and fish can teach what Job now declares: the Lord's hand has done all this, and in His hand is the soul of every living thing. God's governance admits no tidy rule: He breaks down beyond rebuilding, withholds and releases the waters, leads counselors captive, makes judges fools, loosens the bonds of kings, pours contempt on princes, brings deep things to light, and increases and destroys whole nations at His will.",
    context:"Job answers all three friends with sarcasm and counter-evidence: the world God governs is far more varied than their tidy formula.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 12:2", text:"No doubt but ye are the people, and wisdom shall die with you." },
      { ref:"Job 12:10", text:"In whose hand is the soul of every living thing, and the breath of all mankind." },
      { ref:"Job 12:16", text:"With him is strength and wisdom: the deceived and the deceiver are his." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's governance is wiser and freer than any formula that equates ease with righteousness.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which four parts of creation did Job say could teach about the Lord's hand?", choices:["Wind, fire, rain, and snow","Sun, moon, stars, and clouds","Rivers, seas, hills, and valleys","Beasts, the fowls of the air, the earth, and the fishes of the sea"], a:3, why:"Job said, ask the beasts and the fowls of the air, speak to the earth, and the fishes of the sea shall declare unto thee." },
      { q:"How did Job use the world's realities to answer his friends' neat formula?", choices:["He pointed out that robbers prosper and the just man is mocked, so ease is no proof of God's favor","He argued nature proves the friends right","He said only his own experience matters","He claimed the friends invented the doctrine of retribution"], a:0, why:"Job observed that the tabernacles of robbers prosper and they that provoke God are secure, while the just upright man is laughed to scorn." },
      { q:"What does Job's catalogue of God's overturning acts teach about His rule?", choices:["Human institutions should be distrusted always","God's wisdom and strength govern all human power and outcomes","Wisdom belongs to rulers alone","Nations rise only through violence"], a:1, why:"Job declared that with God is wisdom and strength, that the deceived and the deceiver are His, and that He overturns counselors, judges, kings, and princes." }
    ] },
  { n:13, summary:"Job has heard it all before and wants no more of it. What they know, he knows; he is not inferior. He wants to reason directly with the Almighty, while his friends are forgers of lies and valueless physicians; their wisdom would be to hold their peace. Sharper still, he asks whether they mean to speak wickedly for God and show Him partiality — for God will surely reprove them for accepting persons. Job then declares his own position: let come on him what will, though God slay him, yet will he trust in Him, and he will maintain his own ways before Him, because a hypocrite cannot come before God. He has ordered his cause and expects to be justified. He asks only that God withdraw His hand and dread so they can talk; then he demands to be told plainly how many his sins are, since God treats him like an enemy over the iniquities of his youth.",
    context:"Job presses on in his reply, demanding silence from his friends and a direct hearing with God while declaring his trust.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 13:5", text:"O that ye would altogether hold your peace! and it should be your wisdom." },
      { ref:"Job 13:15", text:"Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him: but I will maintain mine own ways before him." },
      { ref:"Job 13:23", text:"How many are mine iniquities and sins? make me to know my transgression and my sin." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Trust in God can hold together bold honesty about suffering: though He slay me, yet will I trust in Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What names did Job give his three friends in this speech?", choices:["Forgers of lies and physicians of no value","Wise men and faithful guides","Prophets without honor","Faithless shepherds and sleeping watchmen"], a:0, why:"Job said, ye are forgers of lies, ye are all physicians of no value, and wished they would altogether hold their peace." },
      { q:"What warning did Job give the friends about defending God?", choices:["God cannot hear their arguments","God will surely reprove them if they speak wickedly for Him and show partiality","Their defense will earn them Job's wealth","The friends will be rewarded for their loyalty to truth"], a:1, why:"Job asked whether they would speak wickedly for God and contend for Him, warning that He will surely reprove you if ye do secretly accept persons." },
      { q:"What does Job's declaration 'Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him' teach about faith?", choices:["Faith guarantees protection from death","Trust depends on receiving explanations first","Real trust can coexist with honest dispute and unanswered pain","Faith means pretending everything is fine"], a:2, why:"Job said he would maintain his own ways before God, that a hypocrite shall not come before Him, and that He also shall be my salvation." }
    ] },
  { n:14, summary:"Job turns to the human condition. Man, born of woman, has few days full of trouble; he blossoms like a flower and flees like a shadow. Why should God bring such a creature into judgment, since no one can bring a clean thing out of an unclean? Job wishes God would simply look away until, like a hired servant, man finishes his appointed day. He notes a bitter asymmetry: a cut-down tree sprouts again at the scent of water, but man dies, wastes away, and does not rise until the heavens are no more. Yet hope flickers in his plea: Oh that God would hide him in the grave until His wrath is past, appoint him a set time, and remember him — then he would wait all his appointed days till his change came, and answer when God called, because God would desire the work of His hands.",
    context:"Job completes this reply with a meditation on mortality and his famous question whether a man who dies shall live again.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 14:1", text:"Man that is born of a woman is of few days, and full of trouble." },
      { ref:"Job 14:7", text:"For there is hope of a tree, if it be cut down, that it will sprout again, and that the tender branch thereof will not cease." },
      { ref:"Job 14:14", text:"If a man die, shall he live again? all the days of my appointed time will I wait, till my change come." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Man's days are few and full of trouble, but God may appoint a set time and remember those who wait for their change.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Job say offers hope after being cut down, in contrast to man?", choices:["A river after drought","A tree that sprouts again","A city rebuilt on ruins","A vine after pruning"], a:1, why:"Job said there is hope of a tree, if it be cut down, that it will sprout again through the scent of water; but man dieth and wasteth away." },
      { q:"What famous question did Job ask about death, and how did he answer it for himself?", choices:["Can the dead speak? — no, they are silent forever","Is the grave better than life? — yes, for the weary","If a man die, shall he live again? — he would wait all his appointed time till his change come","Does judgment follow death? — no one knows anything"], a:2, why:"Job asked if a man die shall he live again, and declared he would wait all the days of his appointed time till his change came." },
      { q:"What hope does Job's plea to be hidden in the grave until God's wrath pass point toward?", choices:["That death is simply the end of everything","That God may appoint a set time and remember His creature, answering him when He calls","That suffering people should end their lives early","That the grave is preferable to any blessing"], a:1, why:"Job begged God to hide him in the grave, appoint him a set time and remember him, and said Thou shalt call, and I will answer thee." }
    ] },
  { n:15, summary:"Eliphaz rounds on Job for the second time. Should a wise man fill his belly with the east wind? Job, he says, is restraining prayer, and his own mouth testifies against him. Eliphaz asks pointed questions: was Job the first man born, does he alone possess God's secret, when the grayheaded and very aged men among them are much older than Job's father? His theology repeats: man, who drinks iniquity like water, cannot be clean, and even the heavens are not clean in God's sight. Then comes his inherited portrait of the wicked man: anguish haunts him all his days; a dreadful sound is in his ears; he fights against God and so wanders, poor, in desolate cities; his branches dry before their time, fire consumes the tabernacles of bribery, and he who conceives mischief brings forth vanity.",
    context:"The second cycle begins as Eliphaz answers Job a second time, now more insulted and more insistent that the wicked always suffer.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 15:6", text:"Thine own mouth condemneth thee, and not I: yea, thine own lips testify against thee." },
      { ref:"Job 15:14", text:"What is man, that he should be clean? and he which is born of a woman, that he should be righteous?" },
      { ref:"Job 15:20", text:"The wicked man travaileth with pain all his days, and the number of years is hidden to the oppressor." }
    ],
    takeaway:"No one is clean before God by nature; that truth should humble us, not sharpen us against sufferers.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"On whose age and authority did Eliphaz lean in this second speech?", choices:["The priests of his temple","Job's own ancestors","Grayheaded and very aged men much older than Job's father","The royal scribes of the east"], a:2, why:"Eliphaz said, with us are both the grayheaded and very aged men, much elder than thy father, and asked what Job knew that they did not." },
      { q:"What did Eliphaz say Job's own mouth was doing to him?", choices:["Proving his eloquence","Concealing his grief from others","Preparing a defense for heaven","Condemning him — his lips testified against him and uttered his iniquity"], a:3, why:"Eliphaz claimed thine own mouth condemneth thee, and not I, and that Job chosest the tongue of the crafty and restrained prayer before God." },
      { q:"What lesson does Eliphaz's confident use of inherited teaching carry for readers?", choices:["Tradition and consensus, however revered, must not pass final judgment on a sufferer God has not condemned","Ancient sayings should always be obeyed without question","The old are always wrong about suffering","Wisdom from the fathers has no value today"], a:0, why:"Eliphaz judged Job by a rule — the wicked always visibly suffer — that the opening chapters already showed was false in Job's case." }
    ] },
  { n:16, summary:"Job calls his friends what they are: miserable comforters. If their souls were in his place, he could heap up words against them too — but instead he would strengthen them with his mouth. Nothing he says eases his grief, for God has worn him out: his wrinkled skin testifies against him, his enemy tears him in wrath, men gape at him and strike his cheek, and God has delivered him to the ungodly, shaken him to pieces, set him up as a mark, and broken him with breach upon breach. Job has sewn sackcloth on his skin, and his face is foul with weeping — yet not for any injustice in his hands, for his prayer is pure. He cries that the earth not cover his blood, points to a witness in heaven and a record on high, and pours out tears to God, wishing someone would plead for him with God as a man pleads for his neighbor.",
    context:"Job replies to Eliphaz's second speech, calling his comforters worthless and locating his witness in heaven.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 16:2", text:"I have heard many such things: miserable comforters are ye all." },
      { ref:"Job 16:17", text:"Not for any injustice in mine hands: also my prayer is pure." },
      { ref:"Job 16:19", text:"Also now, behold, my witness is in heaven, and my record is on high." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When friends misjudge us, heaven still holds a witness who pleads our cause.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What phrase did Job use to describe his three friends?", choices:["Faithful physicians","Miserable comforters","Loud thunderers","Wounded healers"], a:3, why:"Job said, I have heard many such things: miserable comforters are ye all." },
      { q:"What did Job insist about himself even while his face was foul with weeping?", choices:["There was no injustice in his hands and his prayer was pure","He had discovered the sin that caused his fall","He deserved every stroke he received","He no longer believed in God's justice"], a:0, why:"Job declared his suffering was not for any injustice in mine hands: also my prayer is pure." },
      { q:"What does Job's appeal, 'my witness is in heaven, and my record is on high,' promise the falsely accused?", choices:["They will always win back their property in this life","Heaven knows the truth of their cause even when friends condemn them","They should stop praying until vindicated","Their accusers will never face any judgment"], a:1, why:"Job pointed past his scornful friends to a witness in heaven and poured out tears unto God, wishing one would plead for him as a man pleadeth for his neighbour." }
    ] },
  { n:17, summary:"Job's breath is corrupt, his days extinct, and the grave ready — yet mockers surround him. He asks God to put up security for him, since no one else will strike hands in his defense; the friends' hearts have been hidden from understanding, and flatterers' children fail. Once honored, he is now a byword of the people, dim-eyed with sorrow. Still he can see one thing clearly: upright men will be astonished at his treatment, the righteous will hold on their way, and those with clean hands will grow stronger and stronger — while among his three counselors he cannot find one wise man. His purposes are broken off; if he waits, the grave is his house, corruption his father and the worm his mother and sister. He ends in darkness: where is now my hope, and who shall see it?",
    context:"Job continues the same reply, despondent about the grave yet certain the righteous hold on their way.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 17:3", text:"Lay down now, put me in a surety with thee; who is he that will strike hands with me?" },
      { ref:"Job 17:9", text:"The righteous also shall hold on his way, and he that hath clean hands shall be stronger and stronger." },
      { ref:"Job 17:15", text:"And where is now my hope? as for my hope, who shall see it?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"The righteous hold on their way, and clean hands grow stronger and stronger through trial.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What had Job become among the people, according to his own words?", choices:["A byword","A prince","A prophet","A watchman"], a:0, why:"Job said God hath made me also a byword of the people; and aforetime I was as a tabret." },
      { q:"What did Job affirm the righteous would do despite his dark circumstances?", choices:["Leave the land of Uz forever","Hold on their way, with clean hands growing stronger and stronger","Accept the friends' teaching without question","Rise up and drive the friends away"], a:1, why:"Job said the righteous also shall hold on his way, and he that hath clean hands shall be stronger and stronger." },
      { q:"What does Job's closing cry, 'where is now my hope?', leave hanging over the chapter?", choices:["That hope is found in human friends","That hope dies with the body","That Job's hope must finally rest beyond the grave in God","That the friends' formula would restore him"], a:2, why:"Job named corruption his father and the worm his mother and sister, leaving his question about hope pointing past the grave." }
    ] },
  { n:18, summary:"Bildad's second speech is short and severe. Why does Job treat the friends as beasts and stupid, he asks, and why does he tear himself in anger — shall the earth be forsaken for him? Then he paints the fate of the wicked in a relentless chain of images: light put out in the tent, steps straitened, the man tripped by his own feet into net, snare, and trap; terrors on every side, hunger gnawing his strength, the firstborn of death devouring him; his confidence rooted out and delivered to the king of terrors; brimstone scattered on his dwelling; roots dried beneath and branch cut off above; his memory perishing from the earth, no son or nephew surviving, and later generations astonished at his day. The point is unmistakable: this, says Bildad, is the dwelling of him that knows not God.",
    context:"Bildad's second speech answers Job's protests with a single grim theme: the total destruction that waits for the man who does not know God.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 18:5", text:"Yea, the light of the wicked shall be put out, and the spark of his fire shall not shine." },
      { ref:"Job 18:14", text:"His confidence shall be rooted out of his tabernacle, and it shall bring him to the king of terrors." },
      { ref:"Job 18:21", text:"Surely such are the dwellings of the wicked, and this is the place of him that knoweth not God." }
    ],
    takeaway:"A life without God ends in extinguished light; confidence not rooted in Him is rooted out.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What hunting images did Bildad say would catch the wicked man?", choices:["Wolves and hunters' arrows","A net, snare, gin, and trap","Lions and bears","Thorns and briars"], a:1, why:"Bildad said the wicked is cast into a net by his own feet, walks upon a snare, is taken by the gin at the heel, and has a trap laid in the way." },
      { q:"Who or what did Bildad say would devour the wicked man's strength?", choices:["Famine and locusts","His own servants","The firstborn of death","The flood and the fire"], a:2, why:"Bildad said destruction shall devour the strength of his skin: even the firstborn of death shall devour his strength, bringing him to the king of terrors." },
      { q:"What did Bildad's grim portrait assume about Job's suffering?", choices:["It was a mystery no one could read","It was punishment Job did not deserve","It was only temporary discipline","It marked Job as one of the wicked who know not God"], a:3, why:"Bildad ended, surely such are the dwellings of the wicked, and this is the place of him that knoweth not God — applying the picture straight to Job." }
    ] },
  { n:19, summary:"Job protests that his friends have vexed his soul and broken him in pieces with words ten times over; even if he had erred, the error remains with himself. God has overthrown him: his cry for justice goes unheard, his way is fenced with darkness, his glory and crown stripped, and his hope uprooted like a tree. Isolation follows: brethren, acquaintances, kinsfolk, servants, and even his wife find him strange; children despise him; those he loved have turned against him. He begs for pity, since God's hand has touched him, and asks why his friends persecute him as God does. Then comes his immortal hope: he wishes his words printed in a book and graven in rock forever, for he knows that his Redeemer lives and will stand at the latter day upon the earth, and that though worms destroy this body, he will see God in his flesh with his own eyes. He warns his tormentors that there is a judgment.",
    context:"Job's reply contains his most famous declaration of hope — the living Redeemer — amid the deepest description of his abandonment.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 19:21", text:"Have pity upon me, have pity upon me, O ye my friends; for the hand of God hath touched me." },
      { ref:"Job 19:25", text:"For I know that my redeemer liveth, and that he shall stand at the latter day upon the earth:" },
      { ref:"Job 19:26", text:"And though after my skin worms destroy this body, yet in my flesh shall I see God:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Because our Redeemer lives, the believer's hope outlasts loss, betrayal, and the grave itself.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many times did Job say his friends had reproached him?", choices:["Three times","Seven times","Ten times","Twelve times"], a:2, why:"Job said, these ten times have ye reproached me: ye are not ashamed that ye make yourselves strange to me." },
      { q:"What did Job declare he knew about his Redeemer?", choices:["That the Redeemer would be one of the three friends","That the Redeemer would restore his flocks in his lifetime","That the Redeemer was a descendant of the men of the east","That his Redeemer liveth and shall stand at the latter day upon the earth"], a:3, why:"Job testified, for I know that my redeemer liveth, and that he shall stand at the latter day upon the earth." },
      { q:"What does Job's confidence that he will see God 'in my flesh' affirm?", choices:["A personal, bodily reunion with God beyond death — seen with his own eyes","That he expected to recover and visit a temple","That visions of God come only in dreams","That death ends all consciousness"], a:0, why:"Job said though after my skin worms destroy this body, yet in my flesh shall I see God: whom I shall see for myself, and mine eyes shall behold." }
    ] },
  { n:20, summary:"Zophar's second speech hurries out because he feels reproached. His doctrine, he says, goes back to the beginning: the triumphing of the wicked is short, and the hypocrite's joy lasts only a moment. However high the wicked man climbs, he perishes like his own dung, flees like a dream, and those who saw him ask where he went. His children must make restitution for his oppression; his bones are full of the sin of his youth. Zophar offers a revolting picture of secret sin: wickedness tastes sweet under the tongue but turns to the gall of asps inside, and swallowed riches come back up. Because he oppressed the poor and seized houses he did not build, wrath rains down while he eats, the sword pierces him, fire consumes him, heaven reveals his iniquity, and his goods flow away. This, says Zophar, is the wicked man's appointed heritage from God.",
    context:"Zophar's second speech restates the short-lived triumph of the wicked with vivid images of sudden, inescapable ruin.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 20:5", text:"That the triumphing of the wicked is short, and the joy of the hypocrite but for a moment?" },
      { ref:"Job 20:19", text:"Because he hath oppressed and hath forsaken the poor; because he hath violently taken away an house which he builded not;" },
      { ref:"Job 20:29", text:"This is the portion of a wicked man from God, and the heritage appointed unto him by God." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Success without God is brief; what is gained by oppression is lost under His wrath.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Zophar say the wicked man's bones were full of?", choices:["Marrow and strength","The dust of the field","The scars of battle","The sin of his youth"], a:3, why:"Zophar said his bones are full of the sin of his youth, which shall lie down with him in the dust." },
      { q:"What was the central claim of Zophar's speech?", choices:["The triumphing of the wicked is short, and the joy of the hypocrite is but for a moment","The wicked are rewarded in the next life only","The wicked always repent before death","Righteous and wicked meet identical ends"], a:0, why:"Zophar declared this doctrine as old as man's placement on earth: though the wicked mount up to the heavens, he shall perish for ever." },
      { q:"What does Zophar's vivid picture of hidden sin — sweet in the mouth, gall of asps within — teach?", choices:["Sin is only dangerous when discovered by others","Nourishing sin in secret eventually turns to poison and loss","Temptation should be hidden under the tongue","Wealth always comes back to the sinner"], a:1, why:"Zophar said though wickedness be sweet in his mouth, yet his meat in his bowels is turned, it is the gall of asps within him, and he vomits up swallowed riches." }
    ] },
  { n:21, summary:"Job closes the first cycle of speeches by challenging the friends' whole theory with observed facts. Let them mark him and be astonished: the wicked often live to old age and grow mighty in power. Their children are established before their eyes, their houses are safe from fear, no rod of God strikes them, their little ones dance to timbrel and harp, and they spend their days in wealth, going down to the grave in a moment — while telling God to depart, since serving Him seems to bring no profit. Job concedes that the wicked sometimes drink wrath and vanish like chaff, but notes that at death the man who dies at ease and the man who dies in bitterness lie down alike in the dust. The friends say the wicked are reserved for the day of wrath, yet no one confronts them and they rest in honored tombs. Job ends flatly: their answers contain nothing but falsehood.",
    context:"Job's reply ends the first cycle of speeches, marshaling evidence that the wicked often prosper to the grave — a direct contradiction of the friends' formula.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Job 21:7", text:"Wherefore do the wicked live, become old, yea, are mighty in power?" },
      { ref:"Job 21:14", text:"Therefore they say unto God, Depart from us; for we desire not the knowledge of thy ways." },
      { ref:"Job 21:34", text:"How then comfort ye me in vain, seeing in your answers there remaineth falsehood?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Prosperity is not proof of righteousness, and suffering is not proof of sin; God alone judges rightly.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What instruments did Job say the wicked use in their celebration?", choices:["The timbrel and harp, rejoicing at the sound of the organ","Trumpets and cymbals only","Flutes and drums in the temple","No instruments at all"], a:0, why:"Job said they take the timbrel and harp, and rejoice at the sound of the organ, spending their days in wealth." },
      { q:"What observed pattern did Job set against the friends' doctrine?", choices:["The wicked always die young in misery","Righteous people always prosper","The wicked often live long, prosper, and go down to the grave in a moment","God answers the wicked's prayers instantly"], a:2, why:"Job asked, wherefore do the wicked live, become old, yea, are mighty in power? Their houses are safe from fear and no rod of God is on them." },
      { q:"What does Job's final verdict on his comforters — 'in your answers there remaineth falsehood' — teach about evaluating counsel?", choices:["Any counsel is acceptable if kindly meant","Counsel that contradicts observable truth is false comfort, however pious it sounds","The majority opinion is usually correct","Sufferers should never evaluate the advice they receive"], a:1, why:"Job showed by evidence that the wicked often prosper, so the friends' neat formula was simply false; he asked, how then comfort ye me in vain?" }
    ] }
);
